  "fromId": "g_palatalized",
  "toId": "dz",
  "preamble": "Assibilation of a fronted (palatalized) voiced velar to the alveolar affricate [dz]. The textbook case is the Slavic second and third (progressive) velar palatalizations, in which *g before or after front vowels was fronted to a palatal and then assibilated to *dz; the affricate survives in the oldest Old Church Slavonic texts, in Lechitic (Polish nodze, pieniądz, ksiądz) and in the Ohrid dialects of Macedonian, elsewhere simplifying to z. Latvian independently turned the velar before front vowels into dz, where Lithuanian keeps a palatalized [ɡʲ].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fronting, Assibilation, Affrication",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Common Slavic (Polish, Old Church Slavonic)",
      "languageFamily": "Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *nogě, dative/locative of *noga 'leg' (second palatalization)",
          "to": "Polish nodze [ˈnɔd͡zɛ]",
          "note": "*g before the new front vowel *ě was fronted and assibilated; Polish keeps the affricate, while Czech noze has z."
        },
        {
          "from": "*pęnęgъ 'coin' (borrowed from Germanic *panningaz)",
          "to": "*pěnędzь > OCS пѣнѧѕь, Polish pieniądz 'money'",
          "note": "Germanic loanwords in -ing- underwent the third palatalization; Czech peníz has z."
        },
        {
          "from": "*kъnęgъ 'prince' (< Proto-Germanic *kuningaz)",
          "to": "*kъnędzь > OCS кънѧѕь (kŭnędzĭ), Polish ksiądz 'priest'",
          "note": "Same development; Czech kněz shows the later simplification dz > z."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Latvian",
      "languageFamily": "Baltic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*g before a front vowel, cf. Lithuanian gérti [ˈɡʲɛrʲ.tʲɪ] 'to drink'",
          "to": "dzert [ˈd͡zɛ̂ɾt]",
          "note": "Lithuanian keeps the palatalized velar; Latvian has the affricate."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Balto-Slavic *gīˀwás 'alive', cf. Lithuanian gývas [ˈɡʲiːʋɐs]",
          "to": "dzīvs [dzîːws]",
          "note": "Same correspondence before *ī."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
